QOA 0.1b1 - QOA sounds for every Amiga
======================================

  1. What it is
  2. Installation
  3. Playing and saving
  4. Making QOA files
  5. Speed and memory
  6. Tested
  7. Building
  8. History
  9. License and author


  1. WHAT IT IS
  -------------

  QOA ("Quite OK Audio", qoaformat.org) is a compressed sound
  format by Dominic Szablewski: about 3.2 bits a sample, a fifth of
  the size of plain 16-bit sound, sounding nearly the same. It is
  much simpler than MP3 or FLAC, so even a 68000 can decode it.

  qoa.datatype teaches the Amiga to play it, in every program that
  plays sounds through datatypes: MultiView, a double-click on
  Workbench, file managers, AmigaGuide, browsers. It is a subclass
  of sound.datatype, one build for every Amiga from a 68000 up,
  with OS 3.0 or later, about 6 KB.

  A QOA file is recognised by its first bytes ("qoaf"), whatever
  it is called.


  2. INSTALLATION
  ---------------

  Copy two files:

      Classes/DataTypes/qoa.datatype  to SYS:Classes/DataTypes/
      Devs/DataTypes/QOA              to DEVS:DataTypes/

  Then type AddDataTypes REFRESH in a Shell, or restart the Amiga.
  Nothing else is needed.

  To remove it, delete the two files and restart.


  3. PLAYING AND SAVING
  ---------------------

  Double-click a QOA file's icon, or open it in MultiView (or any
  other program that plays sounds through datatypes).

  What you hear depends on the system's sound.datatype:

  - OS 3.5, 3.9 and 3.2 (sound.datatype 44 and later): stereo
    stays stereo. A file with more than two channels plays its
    first two, front left and right.
  - OS 3.0 and 3.1 (sound.datatype 40): no stereo there, so all
    the channels are mixed into one.

  Paula, the Amiga's sound chip, plays at most about 28 kHz. A
  faster file (44.1 or 48 kHz) is averaged down to half the rate,
  or a third or a quarter for even faster ones, while it is
  decoded, so it plays at the right speed and pitch instead of
  slow. Paula plays 8-bit sound: the 16-bit samples are rounded to
  8 bits.

  The datatype gives the sound its file's name, as the system's
  8svx.datatype does.

  Saving: MultiView's Project > Save As writes the sound as an
  8SVX file, with the right rate and length in its header (OS 3.5
  and later).

  Not played:
  - "streaming" QOA files, whose header gives no length (their
    rate and channels may change on the way)
  - files with more than 8 channels

  A damaged file plays up to the damage. A file that only says it
  is long cannot make the datatype ask for more memory than the
  file could hold.


  4. MAKING QOA FILES
  -------------------

  With the reference converter qoaconv from
  github.com/phoboslab/qoa on a PC. It reads WAV (and MP3 and FLAC
  when built with dr_libs). ffmpeg plays QOA but does not write it.

  For the Amiga, encode at 22,050 Hz or lower: Paula cannot play
  more anyway, the file is half the size, and the Amiga opens it
  twice as fast. Mono halves it again. qoaconv keeps the rate it
  is given, so make the WAV first:

      ffmpeg -i song.flac -ar 22050 song.wav
      qoaconv song.wav song.qoa

  (Add -ac 1 to the ffmpeg line for mono.)


  5. SPEED AND MEMORY
  -------------------

  Like every sound datatype, qoa.datatype decodes the whole sound
  before it starts to play. Every sample has to be decoded, at any
  playback rate: QOA predicts each sample from the four before it.
  The per-sample loop is hand-written 68000 assembly, used on
  every 68k; the C loop takes over in the rare places where its
  16-bit shortcut would not be exact.

  Opening a sound, the datatype's own part, on the real A1200
  (PiStorm with Emu68, OS 3.2):

    Sound                       Time
    10 s, 44.1 kHz stereo       0.14 s
    60 s, 44.1 kHz stereo       0.78 s
    5 s, 22 kHz mono            0.02 s

  On that machine the decoder alone does 11 million samples a
  second. A plain 68000 or 68020 is much slower: there a sound
  takes longer to open than to play, the more so at 44.1 kHz in
  stereo. Not yet measured on a real 68000 or 68020; in FS-UAE
  (an emulator, not real hardware) a 7 MHz A600 decoded about
  7,600 samples a second, and 24 seconds of 22 kHz stereo took
  about two and a half minutes to open. On such Amigas use short
  sounds at 22 kHz or lower, in mono if you can.

  Memory. The file is read a frame (at most 16 KB) at a time and
  decoded straight into the 8-bit sound. Only that is held: about
  2.5 times the file's size in stereo, half that in mono.


  6. TESTED
  ---------

  - The decoder, on the PC: every sample compared with the
    reference decoder (qoa.h) on about 1,100 files: sine, noise,
    sweeps, a clipped square and mixes at 8-48 kHz and 96 kHz, 1-8
    channels, lengths around the slice and frame sizes; and
    damaged copies (bytes changed, cut short, wrong length,
    channels, rate, frame size). With the address and
    undefined-behaviour checkers on.
  - On the Amiga: six test sounds opened through datatypes and
    their 8-bit samples compared with the PC's, byte for byte: OS
    3.2.1 on a 68020 and a 68000 A600 with OS 3.1 (both in FS-UAE),
    and the real A1200 (Emu68, OS 3.2.3). The assembly loop gives
    the same samples as the C loop on all three. Opening and
    closing three times loses no memory. MultiView plays and saves
    them; a file that is not QOA is refused.
  - Music played and recorded in FS-UAE on a 68000 with OS 3.1, a
    68020 with OS 3.1 and with 3.2.1, and a 68030 with 3.2.1.


  7. BUILDING
  -----------

  With bebbo's m68k-amigaos-gcc: make in the qoa folder gives
  build/qoa.datatype and build/QOA. make check compares the
  decoder with the reference one on the PC (needs ffmpeg;
  tests/ref/qoa.h, MIT, Dominic Szablewski).


  8. HISTORY
  ----------

  0.1b1 (17.10.2026)
    The first version: a sound.datatype subclass, stereo on
    sound.datatype 44 and later, mono on 3.0/3.1, sound above
    28 kHz averaged down, the per-sample loop in 68000 assembly,
    every frame checked.


